// Regression fixture for final-review Critical 2 (memory-audit-4e
// whole-branch review, probe P6): the same LOCAL text pushed twice into a
// LOCAL list of text -- the purely-local shape of the same class C1's
// fixtures cover for globals. Before the fix, `p6L` (fresh init, only ever
// a push receiver) qualified for a scope-exit element-free loop, but that
// loop would free `p6X`'s handle twice, once per element slot: SIGSEGV at
// handler scope exit (a Mac composition of this would corrupt the heap
// MID-RUN, not just at process exit, since this fires on ordinary handler
// return, not `cl_free_globals`'s atexit path). The fix (one rule in
// `lowEscapeWalkMethodCall`: a push/add/unshift value that fails
// `lowEscapeFreshInit` disqualifies the receiver too) disqualifies `p6L`
// the first time `p6X` -- a bare identifier, never fresh regardless of its
// own initializer -- is pushed. Expected strict-mode leak count: 4 (see
// ../p6_dup_push_local.leaks) -- `p6X`'s text (struct box + data handle, 2
// blocks; already disqualified by the existing push-argument handoff rule)
// plus `p6L`'s own list (struct box + backing-array handle holding two
// pointers to that one shared handle, 2 blocks).

func runP6DupPushLocal() {
    var p6L: list of text
    var p6X: text = "dup"
    p6L.push(p6X)
    p6L.push(p6X)
    alert("p6 done")
}
